hacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(3) & -2(2) \\\\ 2(3) & 2(2)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(3)^{2}-(2)^{2}-4 \\\\ (3)^{2}+(2)^{2}-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}12 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{12\\cdot 4-(-4)\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}60 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}2.167 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256718" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256718" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.167"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256718" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256718" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-xy-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-xy-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-xy-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-x+y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-x+y-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 1-x \\\\ 2x-1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 1-x \\\\ 2x-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-xy-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x-y & 1-x \\\\ 2x-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-xy-3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(2)-(1) & 1-(2) \\\\ 2(2)-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}+(1)-(2)(1)-3 \\\\ (2)^{2}-(2)+(1)-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.833 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.833"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[2,5]\\times [0,5]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[5,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[5,\\,5]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2.727$ |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y-20) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y-20)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(5) & 2(5) \\\\ 2(5) & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(5)^{2}+(5)^{2}-25 \\\\ (5)^{2}-5-20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{10\\cdot (-1)-10\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{110}[\\begin{aligned}-25 \\\\ -250\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{105}{22} \\\\ \\frac{30}{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}4.773 \\\\ 2.727\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.773"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,-1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1.25,\\,0.25]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $1.095$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+y-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3(0.25) & 3(0.25)^{2}+3(1.25) \\\\ 3(1.25)^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1.25+(0.25)^{3}+3(1.25)(0.25)-4 \\\\ (1.25)^{3}+0.25-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1.75 & 3.9375 \\\\ 4.6875 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1.796875 \\\\ 0.203125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{1.75\\cdot 1-3.9375\\cdot 4.6875}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3.9375 \\\\ -4.6875 & 1.75\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1.796\\,875 \\\\ 0.203\\,125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{16.707\\,031…}[\\begin{aligned}-2.596\\,679… \\\\ 8.778\\,320…\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.095 \\\\ 0.775\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256603" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256603" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.775"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,1]\\times [-8,8]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[0,\\,4]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y-x-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2y+2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}(4)-1 & (0)^{3} \\\\ (4)^{2} & 2(0)(4)-2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{3}(4)-0-1 \\\\ (0)(4)^{2}-2(4)+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 16 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 16}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256624" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256624" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256624" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256624" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,0]\\times [0,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-3,\\,3]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $-2.621$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-3)(3)^{3}-1 & 3(-3)^{2}(3)^{2}+4(3)^{3} \\\\ 3(-3)^{2}-2(3) & -2(-3)+2(3)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{2}(3)^{3}-(-3)+{3}^{4}-3 \\\\ (-3)^{3}-2(-3)(3)+{3}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-163 & 351 \\\\ 21 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}324 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-163)\\cdot 12-351\\cdot 21}[\\begin{aligned}12 & -351 \\\\ -21 & -163\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}324 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{9327}[\\begin{aligned}3537 \\\\ -6967\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.621 \\\\ 2.253\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256610" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256610" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.253"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[2,\\,2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration.\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-12$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-8$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $1.9$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-xy+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-xy+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(2)^{3} & -3(2)^{2} \\\\ 2(2)-2 & -2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4}-{2}^{3} \\\\ {2}^{2}-(2)(2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}32 & -12 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.9 \\\\ 2.4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256586" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256586" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"ques