2db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-5y+8 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-1}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $2$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y-5y+8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-5y+8) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}-5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}-5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-5y+8 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y & {x}^{3}-5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-5y+8 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}(1) & (1)^{3}-5 \\\\ (1)^{2} & 2(1)(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}(1)-5(1)+8 \\\\ (1)(1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256287" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256287" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256287" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256287" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[-2,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $-2$ | $-6$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-1}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $-1$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+2(1) & 2(-2)-2(1) \\\\ {1}^{2}-1 & 2(-2)(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+2(-2)(1)-{1}^{2}+1 \\\\ (-2)(1)^{2}-(-2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256549" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256549" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256549" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256549" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256549" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256549" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-3}} | $3$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | $0.833$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(1) & 2(1) \\\\ 3(1)^{2} & 3(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-2 \\\\ (1)^{3}+(1)^{3}-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.833 \\\\ 0.833\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-255856" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.833"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[1,\\,0]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-3(0) & 2(0)-3(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 3(0)^{2}\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3}-3(1)(0)+{0}^{2}-1 \\\\ {1}^{2}+{0}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "178dfc7769acdecadf0b8bb9a87eb5c69d9b1f5e8baa97ebfe309f6db6b36f4b"} {"question" "q-256548" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256548" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256548" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256548" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256548" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "q-256548" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/state.json" "file_sha256" "819ab0222db3e3b1726ac053c7096d647020e50e8fd0826cda532eac644469e7"} {"question" "e-16569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2] \\times [0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/0f5dda7937b1ffed646730040efe11464d46ef1f3ec6ae5dacf84075bd5be01b.png)\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/example-16569.json" "file_sha256" "ba55f09b92aee93dae80558299b81780f3ecdf1b6079672fce95e298617a6bdb"} {"question" "e-16569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/c3ec90119193ca9c0cb886bc7b7c698eb155e6a801fd267c0a17a0d329eff84d.png)\n\nStarting with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/eff9e5b8ab46002bd82fa42d59f60b1bb78ca04228d7c73b52d131d222ccb894.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-x-2 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1)(2)-1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2)-1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}(2)-1-2 \\\\ 1(2)^{2}-2+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.2 \\\\ -0.6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/example-16569.json" "file_sha256" "ba55f09b92aee93dae80558299b81780f3ecdf1b6079672fce95e298617a6bdb"} {"question" "e-16570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3] \\times [-2,0] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/891b79a977ea7d9f4062d3614d7001ade71cfc10de54fcbae6608eebed7e8d1a.png)\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 0 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf{x}_1 = \\big[ 3, \\: -1 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/example-16570.json" "file_sha256" "72606e1d2303dd9e9d2ca3138894f84331229ca566f265fcd7172e454d0772e2"} {"question" "e-16570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/95e234c5c9b32e1ea965f1cd184563699fbd2793d51a9d1e4ec96ffb4e42dc50.png)\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-4 \\\\ x+{y}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(3) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(-1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}+(-1)-4 \\\\ 3+(-1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{30}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{13}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}2.308 \\\\ -0.846\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/example-16570.json" "file_sha256" "72606e1d2303dd9e9d2ca3138894f84331229ca566f265fcd7172e454d0772e2"} {"question" "e-16571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/c3c2b2e7a9d5b2b8f76499b0ad62d628f9d79bc7c41b6bcaf9926df4f368cdbf.png)\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T,$ and compute the components of the first iteration." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/example-16571.json" "file_sha256" "1d8531cb24e90bee3f0249fffef8da2d7036810db8e1e1ae1291b9024ad0104e"} {"question" "e-16571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\nJ_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1}-\\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/13f9eb9850462317be5a5c7da22fe53ee89b24d9ce1a9226c2d0ee1e6355ba19.png)\n\nStarting with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/8bdb55b26b462507d9810675499558be66e7b970d2f9fdb78e708807e617ce8d.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2(1) & -2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 2(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-(1)^{2} \\\\ 2(1)(1)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/assets/d5eb5a91fdd96bf6ca50539d2119a97ce9e5600b3114f708cda4d95a047e8b81.png) Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.75 \\\\ 0.75\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074434/example-16571.json" "file_sha256" "1d8531cb24e90bee3f0249fffef8da2d7036810db8e1e1ae1291b9024ad0104e"}] "usage" {"q-256287" [] "e-16570" [] "q-256229" [] "q-255856" [] "e-16571" [] "q-256200" [] "e-16569" []} "basis" 2730 "retractions" [[17592186204446 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[- 2, 0] \\cdot [- 2, 0]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}xy - 1 \\\\ y^{3} + 3xy^{4} + 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with $x_{0} = [- 1,-1]^{T}$. Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: x_{1} \\approx []$\n{{field-1}}\n$[MATH:]]$$-0.917$"] [17592186204448 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [0, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y - xy - 3 \\\\ x^{2} - x + y - 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $x_{0} = [1, 0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $x_{1} = [2, 1]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: x_{2} \\approx []$\n{{field-1}}\n$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-2}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"alternative-to-newtons-method-for-multivariable-functions-using-linear-systems\"></a>"] [17592186204450 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 1] \\cdot [1, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{3}y - 5y + 8 \\\\ xy^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1, 1]^{T}$.$[MATH: []$$3$$-4$$[MATH:](x_{1} - x_{0}) = []$\n{{field-1}}\n$[MATH:].]$\n{{field-2}}\n\n{{field-3}}\n$2$"] [17592186204442 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}2x^{2} + y^{2} - 2 \\\\ x^{3} + y^{3} - 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1, 1]^{T}$, and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: []$\n{{field-1}}\n$2$$[MATH:](x_{1} - x_{0}) = []$\n{{field-2}}\n$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-3}}\n$3$$-1$\n$[MATH: x_{1} \\approx []$$0.833$$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-4}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"derivation-of-the-formula\"></a>"] [17592186203770 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2] \\times [0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2}y - x - 2 \\\\ xy^{2} - y + 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T$."] [17592186203770 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y - x - 2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}y - x - 2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy^{2} - y + 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy^{2} - y + 1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2xy - 1 & x^{2} \\\\ y^{2} & 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2xy - 1 & x^{2} \\\\ y^{2} & 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\begin{bmatrix}2xy - 1 & x^{2} \\\\ y^{2} & 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2}y - x - 2 \\\\ xy^{2} - y + 1\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(2) - 1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2) - 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}(2) - 1 - 2 \\\\ 1(2)^{2} - 2 + 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{3 \\cdot 3 - 4 \\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2.2 \\\\ -0.6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203771 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3] \\times [-2,0] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y - 4 \\\\ x + y^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 0 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf{x}_1 = \\big[3, \\: -1 \\big]^T$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)."] [17592186203771 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y - 4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y - 4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y^{2} - 3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y^{2} - 3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y - 4 \\\\ x + y^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2(3) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(-1)\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3^{2} + (-1) - 4 \\\\ 3 + (-1)^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} - (1)/(6 \\cdot (-2) - 1 \\cdot 1)\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{30}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{13}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}2.308 \\\\ -0.846\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186203772 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - y^{2} \\\\ 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T$, and compute the components of the first iteration."] [17592186203772 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\nJ_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1}-\\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} - y^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} - y^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy - 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy - 1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - y^{2} \\\\ 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2(1) & -2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2} - (1)^{2} \\\\ 2(1)(1) - 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix}$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} + \\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.75 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [[17592186202923 :answer-field/id] [17592186202923 :answer-field/key] [17592186202923 :answer-field/type] [17592186202924 :answer-field/id] [17592186202924 :answer-field/key] [17592186202924 :answer-field/type] [17592186202925 :answer-field/id] [17592186202925 :answer-field/key] [17592186202925 :answer-field/type] [17592186202926 :answer-field/id] [17592186202926 :answer-field/key] [17592186202926 :answer-field/type] [17592186202954 :answer-field/id] [17592186202954 :answer-field/key] [17592186202954 :answer-field/type] [17592186202956 :answer-field/id] [17592186202956 :answer-field/key] [17592186202956 :answer-field/type] [17592186202957 :answer-field/id] [17592186202957 :answer-field/key] [17592186202957 :answer-field/type] [17592186202964 :answer-field/id] [17592186202964 :answer-field/key] [17592186202964 :answer-field/type] [17592186202965 :answer-field/id] [17592186202965 :answer-field/key] [17592186202965 :answer-field/type] [17592186202966 :answer-field/id] [17592186202966 :answer-field/key] [17592186202966 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
